{"id": "L10", "command": "submit", "inputs": {"recommendation": {"decision": "keep", "challenger": null, "diagnosis": "Markets were calm this period, especially in MX where volatility sits near the bottom of its multi-year range, and the champion's error kept falling relative to the prior half-year. It slightly over-forecasts margins in BR and MX, and BR remains the hardest corridor. None of the tested alternatives beat it by more than noise.", "evidence": [{"claim": "The champion's error over the last 26 weeks was 45.25 bps vs 63.15 bps before (-28.35%), so it improved.", "ledger_ids": ["L02"]}, {"claim": "The champion over-forecasts in BR (bias -11.9 bps) and MX (bias -7.55 bps), while IN is close to unbiased (2.22 bps).", "ledger_ids": ["L03"]}, {"claim": "The best challenger, a shorter halflife, reached 44.77 bps vs the champion's 45.25 bps (-1.06%), a gain inside the range that is often luck.", "ledger_ids": ["L08"]}, {"claim": "Refitting weekly (-0.2%) and a linear ridge model (0.0%) matched the champion but did not beat it.", "ledger_ids": ["L04", "L09"]}, {"claim": "A shorter one-year training window was worse (+5.41%), and the EWMA baseline was worse too (+6.74%).", "ledger_ids": ["L05", "L07"]}], "risks": "If volatility returns to MX or BR, the champion's long training window and slow monthly refit could adapt too slowly, and the small edge of a shorter halflife could turn out to be real. The persistent over-forecast in BR and MX could widen if markups keep falling in MX.", "watch_next_week": "Check whether the BR and MX bias persists or grows, whether MX volatility rises from its lows, and whether the shorter-halflife variant keeps a consistent edge across all three corridors."}}, "result": {"accepted": true, "errors": [], "attempt": 1}, "time": "2026-11-15T11:45:53Z"}
